@@s001 Divide and conquer: the method
@@s002 ora:mid of this step,dato:interval lo..hi,no:part discarded,ok:found
@@s003 Sorted array, I search for x = 15. I cannot read everything: each step must <b>discard half</b> of the interval.
@@s004 <code>lo = 0, hi = 7</code>. Centre <code>mid = (0 + 7)/2 = 3</code>: A[3] = 7 &lt; 15. The target is on the right: I discard the left half, <code>lo = 4</code>.
@@s005 New centre <code>mid = (4 + 7)/2 = 5</code>: A[5] = 11 &lt; 15. I discard again: <code>lo = 6</code>.
@@s006 Centre <code>mid = 6</code>: A[6] = 15 = x. <b>Found</b> in 3 steps. With n = 8 the maximum is ⌈log₂ 8⌉ = 3.
@@s007 Divide and conquer: the method
@@s008 Divide and conquer (divide and conquer) splits the problem into smaller independent parts of the same type, solves them recursively and recombines the results. The cost is read from the recurrence with the master theorem.
@@s009 The method in six steps
@@s010 <b>Divide</b>: halves, quadrants, left and right of a line.
@@s011 <b>Invariant</b>: why the answer lies in the chosen part, or how you recombine the parts.
@@s012 <b>Base case</b>: <code>n ≤ 1</code> (or <code>n ≤ 2</code>), written explicitly.
@@s013 <b>Cost</b> of dividing and recombining: usually <code>O(n^c)</code>.
@@s014 <b>Recurrence</b>: <code>T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(n^c)</code>.
@@s015 <b>Master theorem</b> (fundamental theorem): compare <code>c</code> with <code>log_b a</code>.
@@s016 Binary search
@@s017 The simplest case: a sorted sequence, and the element to search for. At each step you look at the centre <code>mid</code>: the order tells you in which half the answer can lie, and you discard the other. The interval halves, so <code>T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1)</code>, that is <code>O(log n)</code>.
@@s018 Signal in the exams: <b>"faster than linear"</b>: you cannot read all the input, so you must discard half of it at each step.
@@s019 Binary search: the centre discards half
@@s020 The master theorem
@@s021 Given <code>T(n) = a·T(n/b) + Θ(n^c)</code> with <code>a ≥ 1</code>, <code>b ≥ 2</code>, compare <code>c</code> with <code>log_b a</code>:
@@s022 if <code>c &lt; log_b a</code>: <code>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</code> (the leaves dominate);
@@s023 if <code>c = log_b a</code>: <code>T(n) = Θ(n^c · log n)</code> (each level weighs the same);
@@s024 if <code>c &gt; log_b a</code>: <code>T(n) = Θ(n^c)</code> (the root dominates).
@@s025 When it does not apply
@@s026 The number of subproblems must be constant, and the extra work must be of the form <code>n^c</code>. An extra work of <code>n log n</code>, or subproblems of different size, require another analysis (unroll the recursion).
@@s027 At the exam
@@s028 At the exam
@@s029 For the time of a divide and conquer: write the recurrence, then the master theorem. Phrase: <i>"T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(n^c). Since log_b a = …, by the master theorem T(n) = Θ(…)."</i>
@@s030 Base case: always write it explicitly. Common mistake: forgetting the recombination work.
